{
  "id": "80544",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Unit Key Equations and Key Terms",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "x5fps6a1s2am6h1",
                "text": "Key Equations"
              }
            ],
            "id": "3428488532",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Coming soon"
              }
            ],
            "id": "xsfpa5muty2r7sm",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "a7gcyssfuvj5cwj",
                "text": "Key Terms"
              }
            ],
            "id": "lq0cocfwbzf6r6r",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "active electrode"
              },
              {
                "text": " An electrode that participates as a reactant or product in the redox reaction of an electrochemical cell; the mass of an active electrode changes during the redox reaction."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2558076224",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "alkaline battery"
              },
              {
                "text": " A primary battery similar to a dry cell that uses an alkaline (often potassium hydroxide) electrolyte; designed to be an improved replacement for the dry cell but with more energy storage and less electrolyte leakage than the typical dry cell."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1313271725",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "anode"
              },
              {
                "text": " The electrode in an electrochemical cell where oxidation occurs; the site where electrons are released (from which electrons flow away)."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1681651380",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "cathode"
              },
              {
                "text": " The electrode in an electrochemical cell where reduction occurs; the site where electrons are gained (to which electrons flow toward)."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3526703477",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "cathodic protection"
              },
              {
                "text": " An approach to preventing corrosion of a metal object by connecting it to a sacrificial anode composed of a more readily oxidized metal."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3177728256",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "cell notation"
              },
              {
                "text": " A symbolic representation of the components and reactions in an electrochemical cell. Also called "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "cell diagram"
              },
              {
                "text": "."
              }
            ],
            "id": "449100134",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "cell potential"
              },
              {
                "text": " The measure of the voltage (electrical potential difference) between the two electrodes of an electrochemical cell; indicates the cell's ability to produce an electric current."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1707569837",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "concentration cell"
              },
              {
                "text": " An electrochemical cell in which both half-cells consist of the same substances, but the solutions have different concentrations; it has a potential difference and produces current due to the concentration gradient."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2921307404",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "conjugate pair"
              },
              {
                "text": " An acid–base pair in which the two substances differ by only one proton."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2017472405",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "corrosion"
              },
              {
                "text": " Degradation of metal via a natural electrochemical process."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1726642726",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "dry cell"
              },
              {
                "text": " A primary battery, also called a zinc-carbon battery, based on the spontaneous oxidation of zinc by manganese(IV)."
              }
            ],
            "id": "4113702042",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "electric charge"
              },
              {
                "text": " The physical property of matter that causes it to experience a force when in an electromagnetic field. Charge can be positive or negative; like charges repel and unlike charges attract each other."
              }
            ],
            "id": "4236614787",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "electric current"
              },
              {
                "text": " A flow of charged particles that move through a material or space; the net rate of flow of electric charge through a surface."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2965705275",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "electrical potential"
              },
              {
                "text": " The amount of energy needed to move a unit charge from a reference point to a specific point in an electric field."
              }
            ],
            "id": "580471946",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "electrochemical cell"
              },
              {
                "text": " A device that generates electrical energy from spontaneous chemical reactions (galvanic cells) or uses electrical energy to drive nonspontaneous reactions (electrolytic cells)."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1542604685",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "electrode"
              },
              {
                "text": " A conductor through which electricity enters or leaves an electrochemical cell; the anode is the electrode where oxidation occurs, and the cathode, where reduction occurs."
              }
            ],
            "id": "225415465",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "electrode potential ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "term": true,
                "text": "EX"
              },
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": ")"
              },
              {
                "text": " The potential of a cell in which the half-cell of interest acts as a cathode when connected to the standard hydrogen electrode."
              }
            ],
            "id": "37fqncz6c0qq3pi",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "electrolysis"
              },
              {
                "text": " A process using electrical energy to cause a nonspontaneous process to occur."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2120349163",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "electrolytic cell"
              },
              {
                "text": " An electrochemical cell in which an external source of electrical power is used to drive an otherwise nonspontaneous process."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1103862308",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "Faraday constant ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "term": true,
                "text": "F"
              },
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": ")"
              },
              {
                "text": " The charge on 1 mol of electrons; "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "F"
              },
              {
                "text": " = 96,485 C/mol e−."
              }
            ],
            "id": "181783770",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "fuel cell"
              },
              {
                "text": " A device similar to a galvanic cell that requires a continuous feed of redox reactants. Also called a "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "flow battery"
              },
              {
                "text": "."
              }
            ],
            "id": "172100045",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "galvanic cell"
              },
              {
                "text": " An electrochemical cell in which a spontaneous redox reaction takes place. Also called a "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "voltaic cell"
              },
              {
                "text": "."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2654623974",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "galvanization"
              },
              {
                "text": " A method of protecting iron or similar metals from corrosion by coating with a thin layer of more easily oxidized zinc."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3371144125",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "half-cell"
              },
              {
                "text": " A part of an electrochemical cell where either the oxidation or reduction half-reaction occurs; it contains the redox conjugate pair (“couple”) of a single reactant."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2977086423",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "half-reaction method"
              },
              {
                "text": " A technique used to balance oxidation-reduction (redox) reactions by separating the overall reaction into oxidation and reduction half-reactions, balancing each separately, then combining the half-reactions to form the balanced overall redox reaction equation."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2015929906",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "inert electrode"
              },
              {
                "text": " An electrode that conducts electrons to and from the reactants in a half-cell but that is not itself oxidized or reduced."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2991639525",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "lead acid battery"
              },
              {
                "text": " A rechargeable battery commonly used in automobiles; it typically comprises six galvanic cells based on Pb half-reactions in acidic solution."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1334665331",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "lithium ion battery"
              },
              {
                "text": " A widely used rechargeable battery commonly used in portable electronic devices, based on lithium ion transfer between the anode and cathode."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3481427851",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "Nernst equation"
              },
              {
                "text": " An equation used to calculate the cell potential of an electrochemical cell under nonstandard conditions, using the standard cell potential, ion concentrations, and temperature."
              }
            ],
            "id": "4270551923",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "nickel-cadmium battery"
              },
              {
                "text": " A rechargeable battery based on Ni/Cd half-cells with applications similar to those of lithium ion batteries."
              }
            ],
            "id": "344584359",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "oxidation"
              },
              {
                "text": " A process in which a reactant loses one or more electrons resulting in an increase in oxidation number."
              }
            ],
            "id": "152460538",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "reduction"
              },
              {
                "text": " A process in which a reactant gains one or more electrons resulting in a decrease in oxidation number."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2126382711",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "sacrificial anode"
              },
              {
                "text": " An electrode constructed from an easily oxidized metal, often magnesium or zinc, used to prevent corrosion of metal objects via cathodic protection."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1049243848",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "salt bridge"
              },
              {
                "text": " The part of an electrochemical cell used to maintain electrical neutrality by allowing ion exchange between half-cells; usually a salt solution that prevents mixing and completes the circuit."
              }
            ],
            "id": "3141698202",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "standard cell potential ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "term": true,
                "text": "E"
              },
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": "°"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "term": true,
                "text": "cell"
              },
              {
                "term": true,
                "text": ")"
              },
              {
                "text": " The cell potential when all reactants and products are in their standard states (1 bar for gases; 1 M for solutes), usually at 298.15 K; determined from the standard reduction potentials for the anode and cathode half-reactions."
              }
            ],
            "id": "hvuu0rgub4jdt8g",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "editor": "slate",
